  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1241 AM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026/ \n\n - METEOROLOGICAL OVERVIEW:  \n\nDue to gusty winds overnight it will remain relatively warm \novernight across North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \nToday an upper-level trough is over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. In addition an upper-level low will track \nacross southern Alberta into Saskatchewan through the day today. Due \nto the dry cold front that moved through yesterday afternoon/evening \nit will be slightly cooler today. Today strong low-level and mid-\nlevel winds remain across North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. This combined with a strong surface pressure gradient will \nbring windy conditions to the area today with the strongest winds in \nGlacier County. It will be mostly dry today. This evening through \nearly Monday morning a secondary upper-level trough associated with \na Canadian cold front will move through North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will bring light precipitation to the \nRocky Mountain Front tonight in the form of snow on some mountain \npeaks. Sunday evening there will be isolated light rain showers in \nJudith Basin and Fergus County. On Monday the upper-level trough \nbegins to exit the area. On Monday it will be dry and cool with \ntemperatures well-below seasonal averages. On Monday it will be \nbreezy across North-central Montana. \n\nOn Tuesday there is zonal flow over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will allow temperatures to warm up a few \ndegrees with dry weather. On Wednesday an upper-level ridge begins \nto build in over North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \nThis will allow temperatures to warm up with mostly dry weather. \nThursday through Saturday the upper-level ridge remains over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow warm \ntemperatures to persist with mostly dry weather. -IG\n\n\n - FORECAST CONFIDENCE & SCENARIOS: \n\nOn Sunday there is greater than a 80% chance for wind gusts of 55 \nmph along the northern Rocky Mountain Front and adjacent plains. As \na result a High Wind Warning remains in effect for those areas until \nnoon today. On Sunday across North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana there is greater than a 80% chance for wind \ngusts of 35 mph or greater. This combined with widespread minimum \nrelative humidity of 15% leads to Red Flag Warnings remaining in \naffect for all of North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana \nthrough 9 PM this evening. See the Fire Weather Section for more \ndetails. On Monday across most of the Hi-Line there is greater than \na 50% chance for wind gusts of 35 mph or greater. \n\nTonight into early Monday morning there is a 10 - 30% for a trace or \nsnow on the mountain peaks of the eastern portion of Glacier \nNational Park. Tonight into Monday morning there is a 10 - 30% \nchance for a tenth of an inch of rain across Fergus and southern \nJudith Basin County. On Monday across North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana there is greater than an 80% chance for high \ntemperatures less 75 degrees. On Monday there is greater than a 50% \nchance for high temperatures less than 70 degrees along the Hi-Line. \n-IG",
